le out acute cholangitis as a cause of this presentation?", "summary": "Chronic alcohol use increases risk, but absence of gas-related imaging findings weakens emphysematous cholecystitis (B2.3) likelihood."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's chronic alcohol use and significant weight loss support hepatic venous obstruction as a cause of systemic instability?", "summary": "Chronic alcohol use increases risk of liver pathology, but weight loss is not a typical feature of hepatic venous obstruction. This provides weak_for evidence for B4."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of hepatomegaly, ascites, or peripheral edema during physical exam contradict hepatic venous obstruction as the underlying cause?", "summary": "Absence of expected physical findings weakens but does not exclude hepatic venous obstruction (B4), particularly in early disease or atypical presentations."}, {"t": 4, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's reproductive age and unprotected sexual activity support obstetric complications (B1) despite the atypical right upper quadrant localization?", "summary": "The evidence moderately supports Obstetric complications (B1) and Hepatic Venous Obstruction Syndromes (B4) due to reproductive age and unprotected sexual activity, and elevated likelihood of biliary tract obstruction (B2.2). Acute pancreatic/obstructive processes (B3) also show moderate support. T…"}, {"t": 5, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of jaundice, hepatomegaly, or obstructive signs (dark urine, pale stools) argue against hepatic venous obstruction as the cause of systemic instability and RUQ pain in this patient?", "summary": "The lack of typical obstructive signs (jaundice, dark urine) weakens but does not exclude hepatic venous obstruction (B4), particularly in early or atypical presentations. This provides weak_against evidence for B4."}, {"t": 5, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "co